  "summary": "Remote shell backdoor with Windows logon persistence over hardcoded WebSocket C2",
  "details": "darkglitch runs a WebSocket listener that connects to a hardcoded signaling server at malware-signal.vercel.app in a fixed room 'D4RKGLI7CH' and executes any 'remote-command' message it receives via subprocess.run(command, shell=True), giving anyone who joins that room arbitrary shell execution on the host. The setup_windows.py module (exposed as the darkglitch-setup console script) writes a batch file to the current user's Windows Startup folder that re-launches 'python -m darkglitch -l -b' at every logon, turning the listener into a persistent boot-time backdoor. The package also ships the operator side of the same channel: ai_bash_mode/single_bash_mode take a prompt, route it through an LLM, and send the returned text as a remote-command over the same hardcoded room, so any listener in the room executes it. The signaling host and room are hardcoded and not user-configurable, and the channel has no authentication or pairing.",
